    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe code placement itself is idiomatic \u2014 co-locating human display with the E.164 rule, reusing the same `NATIONAL_LENGTH` table so the country-code/national split can't drift from the validation split, mirrors the `CustomerCode.format`/`regionOf` and `IdentityKey` co-location idiom. Routing it through a mapper `@Mapping` expression matches how `CustomerCode.regionOf` is already wired. So the new method is in the right unit.\n\nThe gap is class-level documentation coherence, and the sibling class shows exactly what's missing:\n\n- **Stale class Javadoc \u2014 responsibility and consumers.** The header still reads \"The single definition of the E.164 form of a telephone number... Shared by `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`... and `IdentityKey`...\" It no longer captures the added human-display responsibility, and it omits the new third consumer (the owner mapper). Compare `CustomerCode`, which explicitly documents both roles (\"Co-locates construction (`#format`) and read-back (`#regionOf`)\") and names its consumers including \"the owner mapper.\" Update `TelephoneNormalizer`'s summary to state it now also produces the human-facing display form, and add the mapper to the \"Shared by\" list.\n\n- **Unexplained visibility change.** The class went `final` \u2192 `public final`, but the only rationale in the Javadoc is the pre-existing \"Kept out of the function classes themselves because OfficeFloor requires each function class to expose exactly one public method\" \u2014 which explains why it's a separate class, not why it's now package-public-visible cross-package. `CustomerCode` documents this precisely (\"Public because the mapper lives in another package.\"). Add the equivalent note here so a maintainer understands why the class was opened up.\n\nBoth are one-line doc fixes that bring this class in line with its own sibling's established pattern; no logic change needed.",
